<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Carrier Battle Group ASW test, 1984</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;Multiple Soviet submarine threats are expected in the path of a CVBG. The CVBG needs to transit the area and defend itself at all times.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Limitations: No off-board sensors are available to either side. No threats other than the submarines are expected. Any contact encountered is either wildlife or hostile.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;Special weapons use is authorized by both sides.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;This is an exercise. The enemy forces are played by US units and no live ordnance is used. The scenario shows the simulated action.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;The exercise area is limited. The task of the CVBG in this exercise is not to avoid the submarine infested area; it needs to fight&amp;nbsp;its way through.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;While this exercise focuses solely&amp;nbsp;on the ASW effort the CVBG needs to be able to fulfil its other missions simultaneously with executing its ASW defence. Most of&amp;nbsp;the aircraft are tasked on offensive missions or&amp;nbsp;distant BARCAP&amp;nbsp;and are not available for ASW defence or close in CAP (simulated by "being on maintenance" in this scenario).&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;The Blue side is the recommended side to play, but the Red side is playable as well.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Scenario designer's notes:&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;The number of submarines encountered during this test is bigger than would realistically be expected during an opposed transit in a wartime scenario. This training scenario can be considered a stress test of the battle group's ASW capabilities, and it also allows for multiple engagements during the course of one scenario without having to reload the scenario.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P style="MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px" dir=ltr&gt;You will probably not find it especially challenging from the blue side, making it a useful beginner's scenario. But there is a lot going on and the goal is to not lose any escorts either. From the red side the scenario is very challenging.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P style="MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px" dir=ltr&gt;A primary goal of this scenario is to allow experimenting with different blue side tactics. E.g., should the outer screen use active sonar or not? What patrolling distances works best?&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P style="MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px" dir=ltr&gt;The scenario loads with patrol missions already set up and all platforms in a suggested formation. This gives a head start for players who would appreciate that. If you would rather set everything up yourself, go ahead and delete all reference points and all missions and start over.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P style="MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px" dir=ltr&gt;The formation is quite dispersed, and if you want a tighter formation you will have ample time to bring your units closer before they encounter the enemy. If you want an even more dispersed formation you can remain stopped while you disperse but you will have to then speed up to reach the goal line in time.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P style="MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px" dir=ltr&gt;If special weapons use is not desired the scenario editor can be used to switch off special weapons use as a side level RoE. Special weapons authorization is set to "inherited" by all units so there is no need to adjust individual RoEs. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P style="MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px" dir=ltr&gt;For extra credit: Leave out the two blue subs, for example by detaching them and sending them due north. See if the CVBG can still make the transit.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P style="MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px" dir=ltr&gt;Created by FlyingBear. If you want to contact me please send a PM in the Command forum at matrixgames.com.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P style="MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px" dir=ltr&gt;Version 1.4, 01/10/2015&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
